  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/06/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_10.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A new character is on the scene, claiming himself to be head of the crime syndicate, one christened the \"Big Man.\"&nbsp; Any who object to it will answer to his Enforcers, which consist of Fancy Dan, a master of judo and karate, the Ox, a slow, lumbering brute of a man, and Montana, an expert with the lasso.&nbsp; Nobody better mess with the Big Man!\n\nMeanwhile, at the Forest Hills hospital, Peter is surprised to see Liz and Flash stopping by to visit the recovering Aunt May, who they heard about.&nbsp; Flash is only along under protest.&nbsp; The doctor tells Peter that his aunt is recovering but that she needs a blood transfusion.&nbsp; Peter is worried about how his radiated blood might affect Aunt May, but as Liz and Flash are there watching, he has no choice but to agree.&nbsp; The deed is eventually done, but he'll be somewhat weakened for a bit.&nbsp; Aunt May soon heads south to recover with some neighbors, but all the while, continues to worry about Peter being all by himself.\n\nMeanwhile, a new crime wave begins to sweep the city, organized under the direction of the Big Man!&nbsp; Anyone who refuses to join their mob operation answers to the Enforcers!&nbsp; And the small fry who are captured by the police know that the Big Man will arrange for their release in short order.&nbsp; The police are doing what they can, but Spider-Man looks for clues as to who the Big Man is.\n\nAt the same time, J. Jonah Jameson isn't a believer in the Big Man, thinking that he's just some character invented by Spider-Man.&nbsp; He heads back to the Bugle and orders his ace columnist, Frederick Foswell, to write a \"series of articles which will <i>prove</i> that the Big Man is none other than <i>Spider-Man</i> himself!\"&nbsp; Foswell is hesitant, reminding Jonah that he claimed just last month that Electro and Spider-Man were the same person, but Jameson will have none of it!\n\nOut in the office, Betty Brant is leaving for the day, and once outside, is met by the Enforcers, who demand the \"rest of the money you owe!\"&nbsp; Peter happens to walk by just then, and is roughed up by the Ox to show Betty they mean business.&nbsp; Pay up or pay the consequences.&nbsp; Betty is too embarrassed to admit to Peter that she borrowed money from a loan shark (though we're not told <i>why</i>).\n\nBefore the Enforcers get far, they're tracked down by Spider-Man!&nbsp; Finally, he's face-to-face with the Big Man and his Enforcers!&nbsp; A couple of pages featuring a brief struggle ensue before we learn that Spider-Man is weakened because he hasn't fully recovered from the blood transfusion.&nbsp; He slips away in darkness and is surprised to see J. Jonah Jameson walking away from the building.&nbsp; He reasons that the Big Man must be someone with money, someone well connected, but <i>nah</i>, it <i>couldn't </i>be Jonah, could it?\n\nPeter goes home and calls Betty to learn more, but she is terrified he might get hurt if he gets involved with the Enforcers, and to protect him, she leaves town!&nbsp; Peter finds she's not at the Bugle office the next day.&nbsp; He bumps into Foswell, who doesn't believe that Spider-Man is the Big Man any more than Peter does, but he gets paid to write what Jameson wants!&nbsp; Peter is discouraged since the Enforcers are scaring all the small-time crooks so badly, none of them are turning in the Big Man.\n\nAs Spider-Man, he's made no progress, but perhaps as Peter Parker, he might arrange to have himself kidnapped and brought before the Big Man!&nbsp; So he announces the next day at school that he's figured out who the Big Man is, and his reputation as a brainiac makes it sound plausible enough.&nbsp; Even Flash pulls Peter aside and tells him that his life won't be worth a nickel if the Enforcers hear about what he thinks he knows.&nbsp; \"I didn't think you cared!\" is what Peter tells Flash.\n\nThe Big Man is tipped off that Peter Parker knows who he really is, and we learn that the Big Man also knows who Peter Parker is!&nbsp; He orders that he be brought in.&nbsp; The Enforcers intercept Parker and force him into a car to be led before the Big Man.&nbsp; Peter is <i>elated</i> -- his plan is working!&nbsp; But he's surprised to learn that the Big Man knows him!&nbsp; Could it be Jonah after all?\n\nThey drive to a large, indoor auto-parking building and shove him into a locked room, giving him the opportunity to change to Spider-Man, who soon finds out that \"every top mobster in the city\" is on hand!&nbsp; Something big is about to happen!&nbsp; And it's too many for even Spider-Man to handle, but he's spotted by the Enforcers and is soon thrust into battle, where we spend the next 2-3 pages before Spider-Man is able to summon the police for help.\n\nAs the noose tightens around the Big Man, he tries to escape with Spider-Man following!&nbsp; But Spider-Man, still somewhat weakened, isn't fast enough to catch him before he slips away.&nbsp; But now he thinks he knows where the Big Man is and heads straight for the Daily Bugle.&nbsp; He finds Jameson nervously talking to Foswell.&nbsp; Suddenly, the police show up and say they've found the Big Man.&nbsp; Spider-Man, looking through the window, is saddened -- he didn't think Jonah was really bad, but here the police are, but <i>wait!&nbsp; </i>They're not <i>after</i> Jameson.&nbsp; It's <i>Foswell</i> they want -- they found evidence in his car, which was seen speeding away from the garage!\n\n\"I'd have gotten away with it <i>too</i>...if not for some crummy luck!\"&nbsp; (<i>what?&nbsp; No \"meddling kids?\"&nbsp; Heh</i>)&nbsp; Spider-Man is stunned.&nbsp; <b>It was Foswell all along!</b>\n\nJameson is crushed, and as he stands there, embarrassed again by Spider-Man, finally admits (to the reader) that he's actually jealous of Spider-Man, who \"risks his life day after day with no thought of reward!&nbsp; If a man like him is good -- is a hero -- then what am <i>I</i>?\"&nbsp; Spider-Man represents everything that's not -- brave, powerful and unselfish.&nbsp; He has to keep trying to find a way of tearing him down because he actually envies him!\n\nMeanwhile, Peter gets a card from Aunt May in Florida -- she's doing fine.&nbsp; But nothing from Betty.&nbsp; And we see that she's in a locked hotel room in Pennsylvania, weeping, missing Peter terribly.&nbsp; She can't risk his life!\n\nNext issue we're promised to learn the \"strange secret of Betty Brant.\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | EnforcersRule | width=28%] From the story's opening moments, the so-called \"Big Man\" is positioned as this month's threat, even to the point where he declares himself the new \"head of the crime syndicate!\" straight to the faces of the other gang leaders, ordering them to fall in line or risk running afoul of his \"muscle\" -- the <b>Enforcers</b>!\n[BR]\n[BR] The subsequent panels then introduce its three members to illustrate that the Big Man isn't just blowing smoke.&nbsp; The Enforcers are for <i>real</i>, and anyone who dares cross him will have to them to answer to!\n\nThough the cover of the issue itself makes no mention of the Big Man, the splash page already clues in the reader that there's a <i>mystery</i> involved, especially when it asks [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"Who is the BIG MAN????\"]&nbsp; (And incidentally, his mask and gangster-like appearance <i>do</i> add a convincing level of intrigue.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Artist Steve Ditko does a <b>great job</b> on his look!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | BloodTransfusion | width=20%] The issue never forgets its supporting cast and even follows up on the events from the previous issue, namely that Aunt May is in the hospital, recovering from the operation she had.&nbsp; Page 4 shows her <i>still</i> there in <i>this</i> issue but now needs a blood transfusion.\n[BR]\n[BR] I find it <i>awfully convenient</i> that Peter's blood type just <i>happens</i> to match Aunt May's, especially given that she is not actually his blood relative (as she was only <i>married</i> to Peter's Uncle Ben Parker).\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>True</i>, the doctor <i>asks</i> Peter what his blood type is (so it's not just <i>assumed</i> he would make a suitable donor), but even if he <i>hadn't</i>, this is certainly one of those times where it's probably best [SPAN: severity=info; text=not to overthink it...]\n\nPeter is actually <i>shamed</i> into going forward with the transfusion, and again, I appreciated the [SPAN: severity=success; text=excellent way Ditko portrayed Flash Thompson here.]&nbsp; And in the panels that follow, we learn that the transfusion will temporarily weaken Peter and that Aunt May will head south to Florida with the next door neighbors, the \"Abbotts.\"&nbsp; (And naturally, even though <i>she</i> is supposedly the sick one, she continues to worry about Peter being all alone and reminds him to wear his \"rubbers in the rain!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=Heh!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=All of these are the wonderful (albeit small) moments that make up the beating heart of this magazine.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe narrative then returns to the Big Man storyline, and page 6 in particular does a great job showing the extent of his influence over the city and its underworld forces, who become ever-more organized under his leadership.\n\n[ASSET: left | FoswellIntroduced | width=20%] Notable in all this is the way that <i>Daily Bugle</i> staff columnist Frederick Foswell is introduced (though we don't learn his <i>first</i> name until page 12).&nbsp; I already commented in my [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12|2026-05-01] review (from last year) about how readers should be suspicious of any seemingly minor characters who is immediately given a name.&nbsp; It was Karl Kort in <i>that</i> issue, and this Foswell character seemed to be cast of the same mold, which I described as a \"storytelling device\" (and even said:&nbsp; <i>I intend to be on the lookout for this going forward</i>).\n\nWell, from his very first panel, Foswell looked to me like this [SPAN: severity=info; text=same sort of character.]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Hackles immediately raised!)]\n\n[ASSET: right | BettyLoan | width=12%] But even in the midst of the <i>Daily Bugle</i> interlude, Stan and Steve transitioned to another mystery, that of the \"other person\" in Betty Brant's past, and she finds herself confronted by the Enforcers right outside the <i>Bugle</i> building.&nbsp; There's even mention of a \"loan\" that has supposedly been paid in full!\n[BR]\n[BR] Again, <i>excellent plotting here</i>, as last issue is referred to a second time on the same page, first with Jameson's Electro fiasco, and now with Betty, who we began to learn has some sort of a secret.&nbsp; Using the Enforcers to follow up on that secret was a great way to tie this month's threat to that ongoing mystery.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=(I tip my cap to the co-creators here!&nbsp; <b>Fabulous job!</b>)]\n\nHer concern over Peter's safety is <i>real</i> (though she refused to admit she borrowed the money from a \"loan shark\"), and when she refuses to admit what she's done to protect Peter, one glance at the page count reveals that [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=we're only on page 8!]&nbsp; (This is getting <i>fascinating</i> in a hurry!)\n\n[ASSET: left | ThreateningALowlife | width=12%] [ASSET: right | NotAtFullStrength | width=12%] Inevitably, the first encounter with the Big Man and his Enforcers takes place after Spider-Man pressures a low-life into revealing their location.\n[BR]\n[BR] Intimidating though they may be to the crime lords, even the Enforcers shouldn't have been a match for Spider-Man, but Stan and Steve are careful to remind us what went before -- namely that Spider-Man has been weakened by the earlier blood transfusion.\n\n[ASSET: left | IsItJonah | width=12%] And by the time he's able to remove himself from the battle to recover, who should Spider-Man see walking away from a battle (that coincidentally involved the disappearance of the new crime lord)?\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=<b><i>Gasp!</i></b>  It's J. Jonah Jameson!]&nbsp; (Could he be the Big Man?)\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | BigManKnowsParker | width=12%] There are simply [SPAN: severity=success; text=too many fascinating developments in the story to enumerate here,] including Peter deciding to announce to the world that he knows the Big Man, <i>and</i> that the Big Man himself knows Peter Parker (another hint that it might be Jameson).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=(I recommend you either read this issue right away, or at least my plot summary above.)]\n[BR]\n[BR] And at the same time, it's because of Betty's mysterious disappearance that Peter is so desperately trying to find the Enforcers and bring them down.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=I'm telling you:&nbsp; not a <i>moment</i> is wasted here!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | Vendetta | width=12%] [ASSET: right | TuneInNextIssue | width=12%] The mystery is ultimately solved (and I'm sorry for the spoilers, which are admittedly hard to avoid while discussing a story that is <b>over 60 years old</b> at the time of this writing, <i>heh</i>).&nbsp; But even <i>after</i> this, the creators pause for a moment to finally reveal why Jameson has a vendetta against Spider-Man.\n[BR]\n[BR] Yet they never forget the hook to draw buyers to the spinner rack next month, and the issue ends with the mysterious secret of Betty Brant.&nbsp; <i>Why</i> has she fled to a \"small town in Pennsylvania?\"\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Tune in next issue!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis is probably the most well-crafted plot we've seen in a Marvel comic to date - <b>every single aspect</b> of the story played a role!\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=It was an absolute joy to read!]\n\nWe're kept guessing until the final couple of pages about who the Big Man is.&nbsp; We even almost believe that Jameson might actually <i>be</i> the Big Man, and as readers, we find that we can't actually believe that any more than Spider-Man does!\n\nThe supporting characters are all there, and all are given good roles.&nbsp; Peter uses his brain to play a part in tracking down the Big Man (by pretending that he knows him).&nbsp; We're left wondering how Betty got involved with a loan shark.\n\nThe Enforcers aren't particularly great in their own right, but they're only intended to be a deterrent for small-time crooks, not to be a genuine menace for a superhero like Spider-Man, and as such, [SPAN: severity=info; text=they play their roles well.]\n\n[ASSET: left |  BridgeTheGap | width=12%] Spider-Man's banter was in character and is not only improving, but now is becoming <i>expected</i>.&nbsp; He's always leaned toward filling the air with snide remarks, but in this issue -- and against so many -- he really lets loose with it.\n[BR]\n[BR] I feel Stan Lee here is <i>really</i> getting him into character, and it helps keep things interesting, even amidst a panel layout that was otherwise lacking in inspiration (pages 13-17 are <i>entirely</i> 9x9 blocks -- I talked about this in my [LINK:1963:ASM_8|ASM #8|2027-02-12] review).\n[BR]\n[BR] In this case, it was Stan's [SPAN: severity=success; text=excellent dialogue] that makes that fact almost an afterthought and helps bridge the gap that ultimately leads the Enforcers into the hands of the waiting police, even while allowing the Big Man to slipping through the hero's grasp.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nTo my knowledge, this is the first time we see organized crime so vividly portrayed in the comics (at least in <i>Marvel</i> comics -- one might imagine that possibly it cropped up in DC titles such as <i>Batman</i> before, but I don't know this for a fact).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: It later becomes such a hallmark of books such as the Spider-Man titles and Daredevil that we really forget where it began.]&nbsp; It's perfectly suited for \"street-level\" characters such as Spider-Man, and this issue's handling of it, while admittedly still in its early stages, did a solid job with its portrayal.&nbsp; A team such as the Enforcers is in character for a mob boss such as the Big Man.\n\nThe only downsides to this issue at all were:\n[HANG] * the cover is rather uninspired and has no background images at all, meaning that if I wanted to slab an early issue of ASM, [SPAN: severity=error; text=I would <i>never</i> choose this one.]\n[HANG] * Everything regarding the Big Man was so tidily wrapped up in a single issue.&nbsp; A fascinating character such as him could have been stretched out a bit in a way that would not have raised the hackles regarding the sudden introduction of Foswell.&nbsp; Yet even so, the co-creators <i>still</i> left readers with a reason to rush back next month, eager for more - we want the next issue <b><i>now</i></b>!\n\nSo... <i>sure</i>... the issue wasn't <b>absolutely perfect</b>.&nbsp; But don't forget, even though I identified a couple of problems with [LINK:1962:AMAZFANT_15_asm0|AMAZFANT #15|2026-04-01] itself, <i>that </i>story still received my highest honors.&nbsp; In this case though, I really do think that [SPAN: severity=warning; text=horribly mundane cover] warrants a slight reduction in grade here, and we end up with a 9.6, which is still nothing to sneeze at!\n\nLet's face it:&nbsp; This was the best issue of Spider-Man since his origin tale (edging out both issues 3 and 9), and all of that [SPAN: severity=info; text=with no real super-villain, which is even <i>more</i> impressive!]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=An early triumph for Marvel Comics, and I whole-heartedly award it a high-grade rating!]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Fabulously done!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<b>Update on 7/15/2023:</b>\n[BR] The letters page of ASM #14 had a letter by Jim Civarra about this issue that I thought summed it up nicely.&nbsp; Some excerpts:\n\n<i>This is the type of story Spider-Man was made for.&nbsp; There was no trite villain (I don't care what anyone says -- comics didn't need super-villains before and don't need 'em now); the story was a real mystery masterpiece (I was shocked at the ending, as I thought Jameson was the \"Big Man\"); and last but not least, some really good earthly criminal crimes were committed.&nbsp; There was no evil chemist bent on world destruction or an alien from outer space slopping up the plot.</i>\n\n(Nicely put - this story still ranks in my mind as the best-crafted plot I've encountered so far - I'm <i>fairly certain</i> that will change, but at this early stage, it's the truth.)",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * Curiously, the <b>Special Announcements Section</b> of [LINK:1963:FF_24|FF #24|2027-04-08] indicates that Spider-Man fights \"<i>FIVE</i> foes at once!\"&nbsp; Hmm.. a miscount perhaps?&nbsp; Did I miss someone?\n\n[HANG0] * Though the magazine itself does not yet refer to Spider-Man as \"Spidey,\" (and you'll note I've intentionally avoided using the term <i>because</i> of that), the <b>Special Announcement Section</b> at the back of this issue's letters page <i>does</i>.&nbsp; (See the Dec 1963 monthly summary for more details.)"
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}